<GuideLayout slug="interior-designer-website-checklist">
    <p>
        A showroom sells by letting people see and touch the product. A website
        for an interior designer or a products business has to do the same
        job on a screen: let the work speak for itself, then make it easy to
        start a conversation. This checklist draws on our work for
        <a href="/work/luthra-interiors/">Luthra Interiors</a>, a Mohali
        business with a wide product range.
    </p>

    <h2>1. Organise products by category</h2>
    <p>
        Visitors browse by what they want: wall panels, flooring, blinds,
        wallpapers. Give each product line its own category page with a clear
        gallery. The Luthra Interiors site is organised across 12 product
        lines so every category can be found, shared and found on Google.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Show real work, not stock photos</h2>
    <p>
        A gallery of real installations and completed projects builds more
        trust than catalogue images. Use good photographs, name the
        project type, and add short captions about the space and materials.
    </p>

    <h2>3. Make enquiries effortless</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>A WhatsApp button, since most customers want to send photos and measurements.</li>
        <li>An email option for formal enquiries.</li>
        <li>A map and clear address for showroom visits.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>4. Optimise images for speed</h2>
    <p>
        Galleries are image-heavy, so compress and size images properly and
        load them lazily. A fast site keeps visitors browsing instead of
        leaving.
    </p>

    <h2>5. Write for search</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>One page per product category with a descriptive title and heading.</li>
        <li>Include your city and service area where it is true, for example Mohali and Chandigarh.</li>
        <li>Add alt text to every image.</li>
        <li>Set up a complete <a href="/guides/google-business-profile-setup-checklist/">Google Business Profile</a> so you appear in Maps.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>6. Keep it easy to update</h2>
    <p>
        New products and projects should be easy to add. Ask whoever builds
        the site how you will change products and photos later.
    </p>

    <h2>Checklist</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>Category pages with product galleries</li>
        <li>Showcase of real projects</li>
        <li>WhatsApp, email and map</li>
        <li>Fast, mobile-friendly images</li>
        <li>SEO titles, headings and alt text</li>
        <li>Google Business Profile</li>
    </ul>

